<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Bourbon</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bourbon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Bourbon was a European royal dynasty that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and, from the early eighteenth century, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. The Spanish Bourbons introduced administrative and fiscal reforms in the empire. Their weakness during the Napoleonic invasion of Spain helped trigger the independence movements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a> and the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon.jpg" alt="House of Bourbon" width="286" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grand_Royal_Coat_of_Arms_of_France.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Bourbons began as a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-dynasty">Capetian dynasty</a>, the royal House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. They date from 1272, when Robert, the youngest son of King Saint Louis IX of France, married the heiress of the lordship of Bourbon. The house then served for three centuries as a cadet branch under the Capetian and Valois kings. The senior line died out in the male line in 1527 with Duke Charles III of Bourbon, who died in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sack-of-rome-1527">Sack of Rome</a>. When Henry III of France died in 1589 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-valois">House of Valois</a> became extinct in the male line, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salic-law">Salic law</a> the head of the House of Bourbon became King of France as Henry IV.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Henry IV was raised a Calvinist and became nominal leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> in 1569. On 13 April 1598 he granted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-nantes">Edict of Nantes</a>, which made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> the official religion but gave the Huguenots a measure of tolerance, ending the religious wars. Bourbon monarchs ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> until the monarchy was overthrown in 1792. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> succeeded his father at four, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jules-mazarin">Cardinal Mazarin</a> ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> in 1648 and defeated the nobility in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-fronde">Frondes</a>. Louis XVI convened the Estates-General on 5 May 1789 and was executed in 1793.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s abdication on 11 April 1814 restored the dynasty in the person of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xviii">Louis XVIII</a>, who was restored again after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a>. His brother Charles X reigned from 1824 until the July Revolution of 1830, and the cadet House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> then ruled from 1830 to 1848.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 1700 the Spanish Habsburgs died out in the male line, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-spain">Charles II of Spain</a> willed the throne to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>'s second grandson, the Duke of Anjou, who became Philip V. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-utrecht">Treaty of Utrecht</a> of 1713 ratified his renunciation of the French throne and so separated the two crowns. Charles III, already king in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, became king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1759. Bourbons ruled Naples from 1734 to 1806, Sicily from 1735 to 1816 and the Two Sicilies from 1816 to 1861.</p><p>The Spanish branch has been overthrown and restored several times, reigning 1700 to 1808, 1813 to 1868, 1875 to 1931 and again from 1975. Since 1733 the Bourbons have been the only surviving legitimate branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-dynasty">House of Capet</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of Bourbon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Crown’s last war ended with the <b>Bourbon</b> victory at the Battle of Almansa in 1707 and the fall of Barcelona in 1714.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>When the childless Charles II died in 1700, the War of the Spanish Succession brought the <b>Bourbon</b> Philip V to the throne.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the <b>Bourbon</b> dukes the minister Guillaume du Tillot promoted agriculture, manufactures and learning, founding an academy of fine arts and the Palatine Library, and from 1768 Giambattista Bodoni ran the ducal printing house, whose typefaces became famous across Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Duchy of Parma and Piacenza · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>On 26 July 1947 Spain was declared a kingdom with the throne left vacant; Franco passed over Infante Juan, Count of Barcelona, whom he found too liberal, and in 1969 named Juan Carlos of <b>Bourbon</b> his heir.</blockquote><a href="/empire/francoist-spain">Francoist Spain · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Philip V’s accession ended Habsburg rule in Spain and provoked the War of the Spanish Succession (1701–1714), after which the <b>Bourbons</b> kept Spain and the Americas while the Netherlands and the Italian possessions passed mostly to Austria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1705–1706 much of Aragon, with Catalonia and Valencia, declared for the Habsburg claimant Archduke Charles against the <b>Bourbon</b> Philip V. After the Bourbon victory at the Battle of Almansa on 25 April 1707, Philip V abolished the fueros of Aragon and Valencia by right of conquest in…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
